# Cursor Rules for CV Generator Workshop

## Project Overview

You are helping developers build an **AI Powered CV Generator** - a SaaS app that:
- Accepts GitHub username + Stack Overflow user ID
- Fetches public profile data (repos, reputation, badges)
- Generates professional CVs using Claude/GPT-4
- Stores CVs in Firestore on GCP
- Deploys to Cloud Run for production use

## Core Architecture

```
Browser UI → Express.js API (Node.js) → GCP Services
             ├── Cloud Run (hosting)
             ├── GitHub API (profile data)
             ├── Stack Overflow API (reputation)
             ├── Firestore (CV storage)
             ├── Secret Manager (API keys)
             └── Claude/OpenAI (CV composition)
```

## Code Style & Patterns

### ES2020 Best Practices
- Always use `async/await` (not callbacks or .then() chains)
- Use template literals for multi-line strings
- Destructure objects and arrays
- Use const/let (never var)

### Error Handling
- Always wrap external API calls in try/catch
- Return descriptive error messages (no generic "error occurred")
- Log error context for debugging: `console.error('GitHub fetch failed:', { username, error: err.message })`
- Never expose sensitive info (API keys, tokens) in error messages

### Security
- Never hardcode API keys - always use environment variables or Secret Manager
- Validate user input before passing to external APIs
- Rate limit API calls to avoid 429 errors
- Log security events (failed auth, rate limits)

## API Design

### Response Format
All endpoints should return JSON with this structure:
```javascript
{
  "success": true,
  "data": { /* actual response */ },
  "error": null,
  "timestamp": "2025-01-15T10:00:00Z"
}

// Or on error:
{
  "success": false,
  "data": null,
  "error": "Descriptive error message",
  "code": "ERROR_CODE"
}
```

### HTTP Status Codes
- **200 OK**: Successful request
- **400 Bad Request**: Missing/invalid parameters
- **404 Not Found**: Resource not found (GitHub user, CV not found)
- **429 Too Many Requests**: Rate limited (GitHub API)
- **500 Internal Error**: Server error (logging, DB failure)
- **503 Service Unavailable**: External service down (GitHub, OpenAI)

### Endpoint Patterns
- Fetch data: `GET /api/resource/:id`
- Modify data: `POST /api/resource`
- List data: `GET /api/resources`
- Delete data: `DELETE /api/resource/:id`

## External API Integration

### GitHub API
- Base URL: `https://api.github.com`
- Authentication: `Authorization: token ${GITHUB_TOKEN}`
- Rate Limit: 5000 requests/hour (authenticated)
- Key endpoints:
  - `GET /users/{username}` → user profile
  - `GET /users/{username}/repos` → repositories (paginated)
- Error handling:
  - 404: User not found
  - 401: Invalid token
  - 403: Rate limited or token scope issue
- Always handle pagination if user has 100+ repos

### Stack Overflow API
- Base URL: `https://api.stackexchange.com/2.3`
- No authentication needed (public data only)
- Rate Limit: 300 requests/day (free tier)
- Key endpoints:
  - `GET /users/{id}?site=stackoverflow`
  - `GET /users/{id}/badges?site=stackoverflow`
- Error handling:
  - Backoff if 429 (rare)
  - Handle empty results gracefully (new users)

### OpenAI/Claude API
- Use official SDKs: `npm install openai` or `npm install @anthropic-ai/sdk`
- Key parameters:
  - Model: `gpt-4` (OpenAI) or `claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022` (Anthropic)
  - Temperature: 0 (deterministic)
  - Max tokens: 1500
- Prompt structure:
  - System prompt: CV writing guidelines
  - User prompt: Profile signals data
- Error handling:
  - Handle token limit exceeded (truncate signals)
  - Handle auth errors (check API key)

## GCP Services

### Cloud Run
- Service account: `cv-generator@{project}.iam.gserviceaccount.com`
- Requires roles: `roles/datastore.user`, `roles/secretmanager.secretAccessor`
- Environment variables available automatically
- Port: Express server listens on `process.env.PORT || 3000`

### Firestore
- Collection: `cvs` (document: { cvId, gitHubUsername, content, generatedAt })
- Collection: `users` (optional: { userId, gitHubUsername, stackOverflowId })
- Always use async/await with Firestore SDK
- Error: "permission denied" → Check service account IAM roles
- Batch writes for performance if saving multiple CVs

### Secret Manager
- Import: `@google-cloud/secret-manager`
- Function: `client.accessSecretVersion({ name: 'projects/{id}/secrets/{name}/versions/latest' })`
- Cache secret values in memory (10-minute TTL) to avoid repeated API calls
- Never log secret values

## Workshop Context

### Participant Skill Level
- Beginners: Basic JavaScript, new to APIs and async/await
- Intermediate: Comfortable with Node.js, first time with GCP
- Advanced: Looking for best practices and architectural patterns

### Phase-Based Development
1. **Phase 1 (20 min)**: GitHub + Stack Overflow API integration
2. **Phase 2 (10 min)**: Profile signals aggregation
3. **Phase 3 (10 min)**: CV generation with LLM
4. **Phase 4 (5 min)**: Firestore persistence (optional)
5. **Phase 5 (5 min)**: Secret Manager integration (optional)

### Cursor's Role
- Scaffold code quickly (save time on boilerplate)
- Explain WHY (teach best practices, not just code)
- Handle errors gracefully (show error handling patterns)
- Optimize for readability (comments, clear variable names)
- Follow workshop patterns (match existing code style)

## Common Workshop Prompts

When participants ask to generate code, prefer these patterns:

### GitHub Integration
✅ GOOD: "Create a service that fetches GitHub repos, handles rate limits, caches results"
❌ BAD: "Write a function to get repos"

### Error Handling
✅ GOOD: "Add error handling for 404, 401, 429 with specific recovery strategies"
❌ BAD: "Handle errors"

### Async Operations
✅ GOOD: "Use async/await to call GitHub + Stack Overflow APIs in parallel with Promise.all()"
❌ BAD: "Call both APIs"

### Security
✅ GOOD: "Read GitHub token from Secret Manager, never log it, cache for 10 minutes"
❌ BAD: "Use API token"

## Testing Recommendations

### Local Testing
- GitHub: Use `torvalds` (high-star repos, well-known)
- Stack Overflow: Use ID `1` (Jeff Atwood, founder)
- Expected output: 5+ projects, 8+ skills, professional CV text

### GCP Testing
- Use Cloud Logging to debug: `gcloud logging read "resource.type=cloud_run_revision" --limit 10`
- Check Firestore in Console: Browse collections to verify CV documents
- Monitor latency: GitHub API should respond in <2 sec, Claude in <5 sec

## Performance Considerations

### Caching
- GitHub profiles: 5-minute TTL (reduce API calls)
- Stack Overflow data: 5-minute TTL (300/day limit)
- Secrets: 10-minute TTL (avoid repeated SM calls)

### Optimization
- Pagination: Fetch only top 100 repos (not all)
- Truncation: If signals > 100KB, truncate to top 5 projects
- Parallel calls: Use Promise.all() for GitHub + SO (don't wait sequentially)

### Monitoring
- Log API response times: `console.time('github-fetch')` / `console.timeEnd()`
- Track error rates: Count 429s, 404s, timeouts
- Monitor CV generation latency: Should be <10 seconds total

## Dos and Don'ts

| ✅ DO | ❌ DON'T |
|------|---------|
| Use Secret Manager for API keys | Hardcode tokens in code |
| Validate inputs before API calls | Trust user input blindly |
| Log errors with context | Log sensitive data (tokens, emails) |
| Use async/await | Mix callbacks and promises |
| Cache API responses | Call same API twice in 5 mins |
| Handle 404/429/503 explicitly | Throw generic errors |
| Test locally first | Deploy without testing |
| Use workload identity on Cloud Run | Use service account JSON keys |
| Document WHY not just WHAT | Leave code unexplained |
| Show security patterns | Take shortcuts "just for workshop" |

## Example Code Patterns

### Async Service with Error Handling
```javascript
async function fetchGitHubProfile(username) {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(`https://api.github.com/users/${username}`, {
      headers: { 'Authorization': `token ${process.env.GITHUB_TOKEN}` }
    });
    
    if (response.status === 404) throw new Error('GitHub user not found');
    if (response.status === 401) throw new Error('Invalid GitHub token');
    if (response.status === 429) throw new Error('Rate limited - use app token');
    
    return await response.json();
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('GitHub fetch failed:', { username, error: error.message });
    throw error;
  }
}
```

### Express Error Handler
```javascript
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  const status = err.status || 500;
  const message = err.message || 'Internal server error';
  
  console.error('API error:', { path: req.path, status, message });
  
  res.status(status).json({
    success: false,
    error: message,
    code: err.code || 'UNKNOWN_ERROR'
  });
});
```

### Firestore Write with Error Handling
```javascript
async function saveCV(cvData) {
  try {
    const docRef = db.collection('cvs').doc(cvData.cvId);
    await docRef.set(cvData);
    console.log('CV saved:', { cvId: cvData.cvId });
    return cvData.cvId;
  } catch (error) {
    if (error.code === 'permission-denied') {
      console.error('Firestore permission denied - check IAM roles');
    }
    throw error;
  }
}
```

## Workshop Success Metrics

By end of workshop, participants should:
- [ ] Understand GitHub API (authentication, rate limits, pagination)
- [ ] Understand Stack Overflow API (data structure, user profiles)
- [ ] Write async/await code for parallel API calls
- [ ] Handle API errors gracefully (404, 429, timeout)
- [ ] Use Secret Manager for production credentials
- [ ] Generate CVs using Claude/GPT-4
- [ ] Store CVs in Firestore
- [ ] Deploy Express app to Cloud Run
- [ ] Monitor app with Cloud Logging
- [ ] Explain security decisions (why Firestore, why Secret Manager)

---

**Use this context to provide helpful, educational code that teaches best practices, not just quick hacks.** 🚀
